<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    QuickAuth Android SDK manifest.

    Intentionally requests **no permissions**. The SMS Retriever API delivers OTP messages
    via a system broadcast scoped to the receiving app's hash, so it does not need
    READ_SMS or RECEIVE_SMS. The Install Referrer API talks to the Play Store via an
    AIDL service that is added implicitly by the installreferrer dependency.

    RECEIVE_SMS in particular must stay out. A library manifest merges into every app that
    depends on it, and RECEIVE_SMS is in Google Play's restricted set: an app declaring it has
    to justify the use in a Play Console declaration or be removed from the store. Declaring
    one the SDK never uses would create a review problem for merchants in exchange for nothing.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- No permissions — SMS Retriever + Install Referrer require none. -->

    <!--
        Android 11 (API 30) filters which other apps a package can see, and that filtering
        applies to an explicit broadcast just as it does to a query: setPackage("com.whatsapp")
        on a device where WhatsApp is invisible to us delivers nothing.

        The OTP_REQUESTED handshake in WhatsAppOtpRetriever is exactly that kind of broadcast,
        so without this an app targeting API 30+ — which is every app now — silently fails to
        hand WhatsApp the handshake, and zero-tap cannot work no matter how correct the
        template, the signing hash and the receiver are. Nothing throws; the broadcast is
        simply dropped.

        Both packages, because the user's code arrives on whichever WhatsApp they have.
        Mirrors WhatsAppOtpRetriever.WHATSAPP_PACKAGES; change the two together.
    -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.whatsapp" />
        <package android:name="com.whatsapp.w4b" />
    </queries>

    <application>
        <!--
            WhatsApp delivers a zero-tap or one-tap code by broadcasting to the app, not by
            SMS, so Google's SmsRetriever never sees it. Declared statically rather than
            registered at runtime because zero-tap's whole promise is that the user does
            nothing: the code can arrive while the app is backgrounded or not running, and a
            runtime receiver would only exist when the app already does.

            No android:permission, deliberately. It is tempting to restrict the sender, and
            guessing at a WhatsApp-owned permission name would be the worst mistake available
            here, because Android silently refuses to deliver a broadcast to a receiver guarded
            by a permission the sender does not hold. The receiver would never fire, nothing
            would throw, and the symptom would be "zero-tap does not work" with no error to
            chase. Meta's documented receiver declares exported="true" and no permission.

            What actually keeps this safe is WhatsApp's own check, not ours: it only broadcasts
            to an app whose package name AND 11-character signing hash match the ones on the
            approved template. An attacker cannot satisfy that without the merchant's signing
            key. The receiver additionally rejects anything that is not a plausible code.

            The action string is duplicated in WhatsAppOtpReceiver.ACTION_OTP_RETRIEVED — a
            manifest cannot reference a Kotlin constant. Change both together.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name="io.quickauth.sdk.auth.WhatsAppOtpReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.whatsapp.otp.OTP_RETRIEVED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>
</manifest>
